A||N||15||jhogans||How do I obtain 200-300HP w/out V8||||1
Z||000000||jhogans||10-26-1999||09:17 AM||||I was curious as how to obtain 200 to 300 HP or more w/out doing a V8 conversion.  It can be a any engine conversion or combo other than a V8.  For example, 2.8 w/turbo, 3.1, 3800, 4.3, 3.4, supercharged, etc.<P>Any combo will do.<P>I'm looking basically for the following info:<P>- which conversion or combo<BR>- ease of installation<BR>- approx cost of install / conversion<BR>- approx cost of engine<BR>- where to buy parts<BR>- HP<BR>- Torque<BR>- Sites w/people who have done these<BR>- ***** All other info, comments, or pertinent info.<P>This should be an interesting topic.  Thanks for all comments and info.<P>BTW, This is the best site for info.  I have learned so much since I started reading this forum.  I can't wait until I find the right Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||10-26-1999||09:37 AM||||I know for a fact that you can get 222 HP actually 221.7 at the wheels ( about 270 at the crank)from a S/C 3800 Series II. In any engine swap other than a direct drop-in it is going to cost you in time aggravation and $ at least 2k$ plus engine and mods. Any body else have more to add?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||10-26-1999||09:38 AM||||meant to add 273 ft-lb torque||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||10-26-1999||10:11 AM||||The GMC Typhoon uses a Turbocharged/Intercooled 4.3 Liter engine:<P>285 BHP @4400 rpm, 350 lb-ft @3600 rpm<P>Engines (with everything) start at around $2500 for a rebuildable.  They are pretty hard to find.<BR>Requires a basic V8 swap kit ~ $800<BR>Extensive modification required to the accessory drive belt system, alternator relocation, frame mod.(welding) for engine mount, many other complications.<BR>Parts are readily avail. including a strong HP aftermarket for engine parts, larger turbos, intercooler improvements, etc.<P>Est. cost over $5000<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||10-26-1999||10:39 AM||||You can stroke and bore out the 2.8 into 3.2, built right they can make 190 to 200 HP. Add a 70 shot nitrous setup and look out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||jhogans||10-26-1999||11:42 AM||||Check out this first car on this link.<BR> <A HREF="http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/Projects/Projects.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/Projects/Projects.html</A> <P>Is it possible to get the HP (375HP) he stated from a V6 and turbo?  If so, DAMN!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||jhogans||10-28-1999||06:32 PM||||Hey guys.  I recieved the following information from Ed Parks from the Fiero Factory via courier, Bozon from rec.autos.fiero.<P>*******************************************<BR>Absolute ease is turbo on your 2.8 (173 ci). Should yield about 200-210 hp. Cost approx $3200. owner installed.<P>Next easiest: 3.4, 207 ci (Camaro/Firebird) with headers. Should yield 185-195 hp, Cost approx $3000 installed. Without headers, about 175 hp,and $2500. <P>Next is turbo on 3.4 (207 ci). Should yield 240-250 hp. Cost is approx $5700, with owner installing the turbo.<P>3.4 TDC produces 215 hp. 3800 (231 ci) with supercharger is 240 hp. Northstar is either 275 or 300 hp (286 ci.). We no longer do these conversions, but he can expect $8-13K.<P>Newer technology can get over 1 hp per ci, with reliability. But that will cost for the conversion. Older technology can get 1 hp per ci, but reliabilty (and cost) is better.<P>Ed Parks, from The Fiero Factory<BR>8710B Hwy 53, Toney, Ala 35773<BR>Shop: 256-420-5391...Hm: 256-882-1568 <A HREF="http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory</A> <P>Simply put, hp/speed cost $. How fast does he want to go?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||four50four||10-28-1999||09:20 PM||||ok, say you rebuild your engine, with all the right parts, say 180-190 hp, then add a turbo, what kinda figures are we looking at now? is there some sort of formula to figure out how much more power a turbo gives? what will this do to reliabilty. wouldn't that be cool, 250 hp (guessing) fiero that lookes almost stock ('cept a few turbo things)!!!<P>one more thing, i don't know a whole lot about turbos, but i kinda get the feeling you have to retard the igntion. couldn't you have an adjustable wastegate mounted in the cab as well as an adjustable ignition, and change your boost to prevent excessive engine wear, as well as be better on gas? then on "special" occations, turn it back up in a couple of seconds (as long as it takes to set the radio) would this work? or do you have to leave it at the same boost all the time||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||10-28-1999||09:27 PM||||Regarding the 3800 S/C there is a blower modification (smaller pulley and some other stuff) that puts the motor to about 275-280HP||||reg||1
Z||000009||88 Fiero||10-28-1999||10:52 PM||||There were threads regarding 3.1 performance...I posted a picture of a brochure from a company that doesn't exist anymore...they punch out the 2.8 V6 to 3.1 and hook it with a Turbo along with the Stage II kit...and the output was 275+HP and was capable of reaching 4.3 seconds to 60..That was Miller-Woods Turbo...||||reg||1
Z||000010||BobC||10-29-1999||12:27 PM||||I can't find rec.autos.fiero or the fiero factory web address...<P>any help, please?||||reg||6
Z||000011||jhogans||10-29-1999||01:08 PM||||BobC,<P>The fiero factory website is, <A HREF="http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory</A>  <P>Some of the service providers may not have rec.autos.fiero in their list of newsgroups.  You can goto <A HREF="http://www.deja.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.deja.com</A>  to read the rec.autos.fiero newsgroup.<P>I hope this helps<BR><p>[This message has been edited by jhogans (edited 10-29-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||fastonpumpgas||10-29-1999||03:54 PM||||all this depends on your price range, i am rebuilding a 2.8 ,(going to 3.2) and I am pretty confident on 225 HP at the rear wheels.  But it is not done yet and I have already hit a grand.  but this is definately the easiest way of all of them to make HP.  There are alot of block mods that go along with the 3.4 ..  And as far as the 3800, you better bring your check book  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>   I have heard bad things so far about the turbo kits, I would say a 70 shot would be nice, but nothing like the neck snapping turbo charger !!!!||||reg||1
Z||000013||jhogans||10-29-1999||04:19 PM||||Here is another reply I received of the rec.autos.fiero newsgroup from Ray, From: F2M6GT@earthlink.net<P>********************************************<BR>I'm in the process of putting a 3.8 Liter Supercharged V6 in my 86 GT. I have to do most of the fabricating by hand. with the balncing and blueprinting of the engine and some Computer Chip tweaking I hope to get<BR>285HP out of it.<P>A friend of mine put the V6 out of a 98 Blazer into a 85 SE he didn't have to do much at all. He built it to 350 HP Plus has 200HP a Nitrous System. A lot of overkill, it just sits and spins with the nitrous on. The Stock Fiero 5-Speed and Axles work great. Just remember to put the best clutch and flywheel you can get in if you add much Horsepower to yours.  <P>You can search Fiero on the web. Most of the clubs have links to parts sites and other people that have modified there Fieros......Good Luck<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||jhogans||10-29-1999||05:48 PM||||Hey guys,  here is another reply I received from the Fiero Technet mailing list from Brian Finley, brian@imi-bottling.com<P>*******************************************<BR>The 3800 V6 Series II (supercharged), which is in the newer Grand Prix's (GTPs), Bonneville's, Buick Regal GS's (and a few more GM vehicles) will put out approximately 240 HP at the crank.  With some money and external modifications, you can bring that to over 300 HP at the crank.<P>There's a company called RAT (Ram Air Technologies) that I buy performance parts for my 99 Grand Prix.  They apparently have a 300 HP (at the wheels) Grand Prix that they've modified using their own performance parts.  I think the Grand Prix is much heavier than the Fiero, so I imagine it would be easier to get to 300 HP at the wheels for a Fiero.<P>Oh, also the stock 3.8 supercharged engine gets about 28 MPG freeway, and about 20 or so MPG city driving.  Not too shabby for all that power...  Oh, and it has 280 lb-ft of torque (stock).  Adding a smaller supercharger pulley will gain you approximately 30 more HP and 30 more lb-ft of torque.<P>One of these days, I'm going to put a 3.8 series II supercharged into my 87 or 88 GT.  Someone has already done this, but I can't remember who it was (I wish I did.. I have a lot of questions for him!).<P>There is one Grand Prix enthusiast who yanked off his Eaton supercharger, and added twin turbos.  He also had 100 HP nitrous shot, and was running in the 9s & 10s ET.  I can't remember the outrageous HP he had the series II V6 up to, but I don't think 375 is a totally unrealistic number.  I wouldn't<BR>want to stress the 3800 series II that much, though, because it may not be able to take it for long durations.<P>Well, hope this helps you a little...<P> - brian finley<P>87 Fiero GT<BR>88 Fiero GT<BR>99 Grand Prix GTP<BR>********************************************<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Terrybogin||10-29-1999||07:45 PM||||When the Regal GNX was still in production, I saw where those 3.8's could be built to over 500 horsepower and the crank assembly was strong enough to hold almost 1000 HP! This really pissed off the Mustang crowd at the time because the 5.0's bottom end got unstable over 750 HP.||||reg||1
